      "content": "Train your staff to watch for physiological signs, such as rapid breathing, sweating, increased muscle tension, or pacing. Verbally, look for changes like sudden shifts in tone, increased volume, or escalating complaints that become less about symptoms and more about grievances. Non-verbal cues are critical; watch for clenched fists, glaring, invading personal space, or rigid body posture. Recognizing these subtle shifts allows for proactive intervention *before* things become dangerous.",
